## Part 2: Hunting for Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)
Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) is a critical flaw that allows an attacker to force a target web application's backend server to make unauthorized HTTP requests. This can expose restricted internal corporate APIs or sensitive cloud metadata infrastructure.

[Attacker] ---> Sends Malicious URL ---> [Target Public Server] ---> Fetches Secret File ---> [Internal AWS Cloud Metadata]

## 1. Identifying High-Risk Target Parameters
Look for application input values, parameters, or API request payloads that accept external web addresses, file locations, or IP targets. Common indicators include:

* ?url=https://images.com
* ?file=http://external-site.com
* ?api_url=https://partner-portal.com
* ?webhook=https://my-integrations.com

## 2. Common Cloud Infrastructure Exploit Payloads
If a web application takes your URL parameter and fetches it from the backend server without validation, try swapping the URL out with restricted cloud management endpoints.
## Internal AWS (Amazon Web Services) & Google Cloud Metadata
Cloud servers use an internal, unauthenticated link layer to let virtual machines check their configurations. If the target server is hosted on AWS, inject this IP address:

http://169.254.169


* The Impact: If successful, the target server will print out internal cloud infrastructure passwords and temporary AWS access keys in your browser window, giving you control over their cloud backend.

## Internal DigitalOcean Metadata

http://169.254.169

## Local System Port Probing
Test if you can communicate with applications running locally on the server that are blocked from the open internet (such as internal administration dashboards or databases):

http://127.0.0
http://localhost:6379 (Default port for Redis databases)

